  "steps": [
    {
      "text": "1. A weight/activation tile sits in LPDDR4 (8 GB, 118 GB/s on the e150) — Grayskull's cheapest, lowest-power memory choice of the three generations.",
      "focus": [
        "lpddr4"
      ],
      "dot": "lpddr4",
      "explode": 0.4,
      "drill": null,
      "stage": "hbm"
    },
    {
      "text": "2. The 2D NoC torus (NoC0/NoC1, row-first routing) routes the tile's packet across the 10×12 Tensix grid toward its target core.",
      "focus": [
        "noc-fabric"
      ],
      "dot": "noc-fabric",
      "explode": 0.5,
      "stage": "noc"
    },
    {
      "text": "3. The packet reaches a Tensix core and is handed to its local 1 MB L1 SRAM.",
      "focus": [
        "tensix-core"
      ],
      "dot": "tensix-core",
      "drill": "tensix-detail",
      "explode": 0.6,
      "stage": "l1"
    },
    {
      "text": "4. The unpacker reads the packet into L1 and converts it into tile layout.",
      "focus": [
        "unpacker"
      ],
      "dot": "unpacker",
      "drill": "tensix-detail",
      "stage": "unpack"
    },
    {
      "text": "5. The FPU (tile matrix engine) multiplies/convolves tiles — 332 TFLOPS FP8 peak across all 120 cores on the e150.",
      "focus": [
        "fpu"
      ],
      "dot": "fpu",
      "drill": "tensix-detail",
      "stage": "tensor-core"
    },
    {
      "text": "6. The SFPU vector engine applies elementwise ops — activations, normalization — on the FPU's output.",
      "focus": [
        "sfpu"
      ],
      "dot": "sfpu",
      "drill": "tensix-detail",
      "stage": "epilogue"
    },
    {
      "text": "7. The packer repackitizes the results from L1 back into NoC packet format.",
      "focus": [
        "packer"
      ],
      "dot": "packer",
      "drill": "tensix-detail",
      "stage": "pack"
    },
    {
      "text": "8. The packet leaves through one of the Tensix's 2 NoC routers toward the next core in the dataflow graph, or back to LPDDR4.",
      "focus": [
        "tensix-router"
      ],
      "dot": "tensix-router",
      "drill": null,
      "stage": "writeback"
    },
    {
      "text": "9. Grayskull has no on-die Ethernet, so there is no chip-to-chip scale-out step here — that fabric arrives with Wormhole. Every result that leaves the die goes back out through PCIe.",
      "focus": [
        "pcie"
      ],
      "dot": "pcie",
      "stage": "ethernet"
    },
    {
      "text": "10. The host CPU talks to the card over PCIe 4.0 x16 to launch kernels and stream tensors; the ARC HS48 microcontroller manages boot and power.",
      "focus": [
        "pcie",
        "arc"
      ],
      "dot": "pcie",
      "stage": "host"
    }
  ],
